# Comprehensive Technical Audit and Comparative Analysis of Three Projects

Audit date: **17 October 2026 (Asia/Calcutta)**. Scope: the three supplied local snapshots. This is a read-only audit; the only deliverable added to the workspace is this Markdown report.

## 1. Executive Summary

These are **three separate applications in the same broad treasury domain**, not three implemented ML systems. Their code, entry points, data representations, and dependencies differ. No shared imports, common deployment, or Git histories were supplied that establish a shared codebase or branch relationship.

| Question | Evidence-based answer |
|---|---|
| Strongest overall demonstrated technical implementation | **Bank Capital & Liquidity Optimizer (P2)**, for breadth of implemented analytical engines, explicit constraints, explainable outputs, and reproducible deterministic results. This is a relative assessment of these snapshots, not production certification. |
| Strongest demonstrated UVP | **P2**, for connecting business-line economics, capital/liquidity constraints, stress results, and allocation explanations in one teaching application. These are established techniques; market uniqueness is not established. |
| Strongest forecasting evaluation implementation | **LiquidityForecasting (P1)**. Actual chronological folds, seasonal-naive comparison, MAE/MASE calculations, and trained SARIMAX code exist. Only the seasonal-naive baseline was reproducible in the available environment. |
| Strongest verified trained-model performance | **No winner can be established.** P1 SARIMAX execution is blocked by missing statsmodels; P2 has no trained ML model; P3's claimed ML training implementation is absent. |
| Largest claims-to-code gap | **CashLens (P3)**. The supplied dashboard generates illustrative forecasts from future synthetic actuals, hardcodes model scores and feature importance, and animates training steps. Its advertised Python pipeline is missing. |
| Most useful Reserve Juggler ideas | P1's validation/forecast/balance/funding separation; P2's explicit constraints, scenario engine and explanation patterns; P3's presentation and report layout. None supports a complete intraday surplus-investment engine. |

**Verification completed:** 39 P1 tests and 36 P2 tests passed in runnable subsets; all 38 project Python files parsed; CashLens's embedded JavaScript passed syntax checking. Eighteen seasonal-naive fold results were reproduced and their MAE/MASE independently checked. P2 base ratios, RAROC reallocation and all five scenarios were reproduced. An independent SciPy formulation reproduced all three published capital-allocation strategy results, without running the unavailable original CVXPY implementation.

**Material findings:**

1. P3's four model cards are constants, not computed evaluations; its forecast arrays explicitly depend on future actual values. Its 3.2% ensemble MAPE is **reported but unverified**, not validated model performance. [P3-html], lines 767–769, 1018–1023.
2. P2's margin solver returned **success=False**, with constraint violations, yet its UI displays the returned margin reduction without inspecting success. [P2-margin], lines 243–262; [P2-app], lines 522–530.
3. P1 accepts missing calendar days and infinity values; this can undermine date alignment and numeric reliability. Its dashboard omits interval sheets from the workbook despite the export helper supporting them. [P1-validation]; [P1-app], lines 476–483.
4. P2's optimization liquidity coefficients use a different funding approximation from its base dashboard: at unchanged scale, optimizer LCR is 247.02% versus base dashboard 169.96%, and NSFR is 109.93% versus 123.33%. These ratios must not be interpreted as directly comparable before/after improvements. [P2-opt], lines 103–126; [P2-liquidity], lines 226–268.
5. No real-data out-of-sample forecasting benchmark, intraday backtest, trained-model artifact, production monitoring log, or deployment acceptance test was found in any project.

All performance labels below distinguish source claims, execution, and independent calculations. A passing unit test, a feasible LP, and a forecast error measure answer different questions.

