## 5. Project 3: Full Audit — CashLens

### 5.1 What the supplied application actually does

CashLens is a polished browser demonstration of an SME treasury forecasting product. Its documentation describes a learned hybrid forecasting engine, but the supplied implementation is a synthetic-data visualization and export application. Its target users are SME finance teams/CFOs; the intended benefit is earlier warning of cash shortages.

Opening the HTML creates 730 synthetic historical days and 90 synthetic future days. The displayed “XGBoost”, “LSTM” and “Prophet” forecasts are the future net flows multiplied by seeded noise. An “ensemble” takes a fixed 45%/35%/20% weighted average. This is executable JavaScript, but it is not learned forecasting. [P3-html], 742–769.

Selecting “Run Engine” advances a timer through training messages; it does not call a trainer or change predictions. Selecting a CSV shows an alert and does not parse or replace data. Configuration dropdowns have no connection to the generator. The downloadable Python action also only shows an alert. [P3-html], 695–719, 1210–1231.

